    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back at your childhood, did you have similar trouble staying in your seat or sitting still in class?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, yeah, definitely. Like, even way back in elementary school, I was totally that kid. I just couldn't stay in my seat to save my life. I was always talking over the teacher, and I'd get these little notes sent home all the time because I kept blurting things out or just getting up and wandering around the room. \n\nMy mom used to get so mad about those notes. She worked the morning shift at this diner, Terry's, right off the highway, so she was already exhausted by the time I got home. Have you ever been to Terry's? They actually have the best french fries. I think they fry them in beef tallow or something, I don't know, but they're way better than McDonald's. McDonald's fries get so soggy if you don't eat them in like, the first five minutes. My friend told me this trick where you order them without salt so they have to make a fresh batch, but the last time I did that, the guy at the drive-thru gave me such a dirty look and then conveniently forgot to put salt packets in the bag. I was so mad. I had to eat them plain in my car. \n\nBut um, yeah, anyway, school was pretty much like that from the very beginning. Just always getting in trouble for being up and out of my chair.",
